{"name":"Aloe Thorn","hex":"#888b73","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aloe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The resins of the tree Aquilaria malaccensis (syn. Aquilaria agallocha), known for their fragrant aroma, produced after infection by the fungus Phialophora parasitica.","Any plant of the large and variable genus Aloe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aloe"},{"term":"thorn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A modified branch that is hard and sharp like a spike.","Any thorn-like structure on plants, such as the spine and the prickle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pierce with, or as if with, a thorn (sharp pointed object)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thorn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aloe","blocks":[{"text":"Aloe is a genus containing 594 species of flowering succulent plants. The most widely known species is Aloe vera, or \"true aloe\", which is cultivated as a source for topical uses and dietary supplements. Other species, such as Aloe ferox, are also cultivated or harvested from the wild for similar applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg/330px-Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aloe"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/500px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/1280px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/500px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Succulent plants, such as aloe, store wate